WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.970
DAVE RENSIN: I am very, very
excited for this next panel.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.060
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.730
My name is Dave Rensin.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:11.420
I am a Google SRE running
Customer Reliability

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.990
Engineering, and I am
super excited for us

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:17.120
to have a conversation
about some

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.480
of the weird, wild, and
just plain wacky things

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.530
that we have seen over the last
10 or 11 years in Google SRE.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:28.370
And really excited to have
the people joining us today.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:29.570
So welcome to the 5th Nine.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.850
Settle in, enjoy your
lunches and your beverages,

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.102
and we're going to kick off.

00:00:33.102 --> 00:00:35.560
A little later there'll be a
little audience participation.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:36.470
I made some notes
because I want to make

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:37.886
sure my introductions
are correct.

00:00:37.886 --> 00:00:40.190
So first, I would
like to welcome

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:41.540
George Sadlier to the stage.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:42.650
Come on up, George.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.210
George is a 10-year Googler.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.020
He has worked in about every
corner of SRE you can imagine,

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:51.800
from ads, to BigQuery,
to Chrome, to Dremel,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.890
which most of you will know
externally as BigQuery,

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:56.630
and we are honored
to have him here.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.130
Next, Steve Carstensen.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:00.410
Steve, come on up.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.990
Steve runs network
infrastructure SRE at Google.

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:05.990
Basically, his team is
responsible for the health

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:07.140
of our production network.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.310
And, you know, when it catches
fire, which occasionally it

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:13.430
will do, they're the ones
who run in to fix it.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:16.910
Our third panelist today,
I thought would be fun,

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:17.670
is John Welsh.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.940
John, come on up.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.220
John's done a bunch
of things at Google,

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.810
most notably working
on cloud reliability.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.840
But the thing I think
makes him most interesting

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.510
for this is his other job,
as he runs a thing called

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:32.030
DRT, Disaster Recovery Testing.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.030
So basically, Steve
and George are

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.010
responsible for fixing
things and making

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.050
sure things are operating
right when they break,

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:40.675
and getting things
back up and running.

00:01:40.675 --> 00:01:42.830
John's job is to
proactively break

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.331
things to make sure that we can
respond in a reasonable way.

00:01:45.331 --> 00:01:47.621
And I thought that might be
kind of an interesting mix.

00:01:47.621 --> 00:01:49.580
So, if you'll
please, a quick round

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.740
of applause for our
panelists, and we

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.390
will kick off some of this.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:56.106
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:56.106 --> 00:01:57.480
George, I want to
start with you.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.340
If that's OK, we'll
just pass a mic around.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.220
I have a "Farmer's
Almanac" in my house

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.620
that I bought lo, these many
years ago when I was a kid.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:09.169
And in the front, it's
got a set of things

00:02:09.169 --> 00:02:10.830
that every farmer knows is true.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:14.510
And one of them is that
lightning never strikes twice

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:15.296
in the same place.

00:02:15.296 --> 00:02:16.670
And what I want
to know from you,

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:20.660
George, is, were they
full of it when they

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.280
wrote that, or were they right?

00:02:23.985 --> 00:02:25.360
GEORGE SADLIER:
I'm not sure what

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:26.630
the science is on the matter.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:30.790
Unfortunately, observed evidence
proves it to be a false.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.240
We've certainly
experienced multiple--

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.830
I'm being signaled to
keep it close to my--

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:36.520
OK.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:37.470
That better?

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.560
Cool.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.490
We've certainly experienced
somewhat multiple strikes

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.110
in quick succession.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:46.780
We had a particular
scenario in the last year

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:50.550
in Belgium, where
we had actually--

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:54.850
it was a half dozen strikes
in fairly rapid succession

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:56.350
over a couple of
minutes striking

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.620
the electrical grid supporting
one of our facilities.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:03.036
Which caused some interesting--

00:03:03.036 --> 00:03:04.660
I mean, obviously,
these facilities are

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:08.255
designed to survive power loss.

00:03:08.255 --> 00:03:10.130
And so yeah, the grid
went out and we went on

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.240
to emergency power,
but it was interesting

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:17.650
because those systems kicked
in, carried the facility,

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:20.020
went back off again because
the power was restored.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:22.180
And then we went back
onto emergency power,

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.730
and then off, and
then on, and then off,

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.117
resulting in much drama
and entertainment,

00:03:27.117 --> 00:03:29.200
mostly because I think we
thought the systems were

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:29.825
malfunctioning.

00:03:29.825 --> 00:03:32.365
DAVE RENSIN: [INAUDIBLE] so--

00:03:32.365 --> 00:03:34.340
[TAPPING MICROPHONE]

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:34.840
Nada.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:35.830
GEORGE SADLIER: Dave.

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:36.820
DAVE RENSIN: That's OK.

00:03:40.775 --> 00:03:41.275
No?

00:03:41.275 --> 00:03:44.245
OK, cool.

00:03:44.245 --> 00:03:45.235
Give me something?

00:03:45.235 --> 00:03:45.970
CREW: Microphone.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:46.720
DAVE RENSIN: Cool.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.230
I'm good.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:52.410
OK, so as I recall,
right, there were

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:53.730
a bunch of strikes up and down.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.430
It was us, and the substation,
and the power provider,

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:57.680
and then back, and then--

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.130
like kind of a
ping pong-y thing.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:01.845
This is not a thing
we plan for, right?

00:04:01.845 --> 00:04:04.050
There is no plausible
computer model

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:07.140
where that pattern of lightning
strikes makes any sense,

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.000
but still we muddled
our way through it.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.940
Can you talk a little
bit, just at a high level,

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.769
about planning and the
general principles of defense,

00:04:15.769 --> 00:04:18.269
in depth, that let us survive
what is, frankly, a completely

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:21.540
unpredictable sort of a thing?

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.490
GEORGE SADLIER: Yeah, I mean,
like any large-scale data

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:26.740
center facility, we've
got extensive backup power

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:28.510
in place.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:32.091
We've got standby generators
ready to come on at no notice.

00:04:32.091 --> 00:04:34.090
We've got the battery
backup systems to cover us

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:37.540
during that transition.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:40.700
We don't expect that
type of scenario,

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:43.285
but at the end of the day, any
power outage is a power outage,

00:04:43.285 --> 00:04:47.320
and we were able to
cover through it.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:50.740
Most of the drama in
that particular instance

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.020
was unrelated to the
multiple strikes,

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:56.560
but more came down to a
software glitch in our battery

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.280
controllers, which caused a
little bit more drama than we

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:03.700
really needed that
particular day, resulting

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.580
in some loss of power to
some racks, which we did not

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:09.017
appreciate.

00:05:09.017 --> 00:05:11.350
DAVE RENSIN: All right, Steve,
let me ask you something.

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:16.300
So the other day, I was reading
my most reliable internet

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.360
conspiracy theory sites,
because, as we know,

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.289
everything's very
reliable in the internet,

00:05:21.289 --> 00:05:23.080
and I stumbled on
something I thought maybe

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:24.880
you could comment on.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.840
My understanding is that they
actually make underwater fiber

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:31.030
cables out of fish food.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:33.130
I wonder if you could
tell me about that.

00:05:37.102 --> 00:05:39.560
STEVE CARSTENSEN: Sure, although
it's not really fish food,

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:42.680
and we don't really
know why, but it is true

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:45.800
that sharks do like
to chew on the cables

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:47.750
that we run the
internet through,

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.600
the networking through,
under the ocean.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.070
Google actually wraps its--

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:54.142
well, according to
stories I have read,

00:05:54.142 --> 00:05:56.100
I actually don't have
personal knowledge here--

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:00.840
Google has a patent
on fiber that we--

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.564
or fabric that we wrap
around the cables,

00:06:02.564 --> 00:06:04.730
that we might wrap around
the cables to protect them

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:07.670
from shark attacks, but I don't
know if we actually do that.

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:09.730
That was a different
internet company

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:12.060
that that story was about.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:15.980
But we have a lot of
animal-related outages

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:17.070
of one form or another.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.160
Now, most of these don't
actually cause outages.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:20.640
They just cause problems.

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:23.300
We had a case in Atlanta
a number of years ago

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:27.890
where a snake got into I believe
it was a power transformer

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:30.830
station and shorted
out part of the grid

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:34.140
at that data center,
which was exciting.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:36.410
We've had birds flying
into transmission lines.

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:42.410
We've had, in one case, a
fiber that we leased in Brazil,

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:47.540
was cut by somebody who was
digging a hole to bury a horse.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:51.380
We've had all sorts of
fiber and network outages

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:53.350
and power outages
related to things.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:01.650
DAVE RENSIN: OK, so cat
videos notwithstanding,

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:04.690
animals are not always
big fans of the internet,

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.009
as it turns out.

00:07:06.009 --> 00:07:07.800
STEVE CARSTENSEN:
They're one of the things

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:09.392
you have to deal with.

00:07:09.392 --> 00:07:11.100
DAVE RENSIN: All right,
John, I've always

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:14.760
wanted to ask you this in
all the time I've known you.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:18.150
So your job, as I
understand it, is

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:21.690
to cause problems for
production teams in production.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:24.770
To break things and either
tell them ahead of time or not.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:27.780
What I want to know is, you're
like the most popular person

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:29.310
at Google, right?

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:33.150
I assume you eat alone,
maybe under armed guard.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.750
How does that work?

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:38.108
JOHN WELSH: I try to
keep a low profile.

00:07:38.108 --> 00:07:41.490
But yeah, so we manage the
Disaster Recovery Testing

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:42.600
program.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:44.340
We call it "dirt" for short.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:50.160
And it's a program to,
in a very controlled way,

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.720
find issues within our systems,
break them, and recover

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:55.320
from them.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.240
And the recovery is
obviously very important,

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.031
and we always want to
do this in a way that

00:07:59.031 --> 00:08:00.750
isn't customer facing.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:04.860
So, yeah, we're
always out there.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.540
We run continuously year round.

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:11.250
And then we have like these
company-wide disaster recovery

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:14.935
testing week events so--

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:19.650
DAVE RENSIN: And
then without using

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:21.108
names, because I
don't want you to,

00:08:21.108 --> 00:08:24.210
tell me in the world of
cloud, the reliability

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:26.220
is not just what
we're offering, it's

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.950
what our customers
are bringing to it.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:31.652
Do we ever do DRT
with our customers?

00:08:31.652 --> 00:08:32.360
JOHN WELSH: Yeah.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:37.159
Yeah, we've certainly done
tests with other companies

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:40.530
in a very, again,
controlled way.

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:43.320
Everything from looking
at incident management,

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:45.930
maybe you have an issue
with your systems running

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:48.760
on our cloud, and you
need to escalate that

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:53.610
into Google support for cloud,
and so how would we do that?

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:58.200
And how do those issues go
from your system, your product,

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:01.464
through to our support, to our
engineers, and get resolved?

00:09:01.464 --> 00:09:02.880
It's something we
want to practice

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:06.787
and make sure we have good
and effective communication.

00:09:06.787 --> 00:09:08.620
We want to make sure
that we can find issues

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:11.880
with your systems, the
software that you're

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:16.260
relying on within the platform.

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:17.700
DAVE RENSIN: Allie,
where are you?

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:20.280
OK, so, we have an audience.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:22.140
I thought maybe it'd
be an interesting idea

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:23.450
if we took questions.

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:25.430
It's always a little--

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:26.940
it's live, so who knows?

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:28.320
We'll see what we get.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.470
If you want to ask a question
about some of this stuff,

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:34.980
or other reliability things,
just give a quick signal

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:37.169
to Allie and we can do that.

00:09:37.169 --> 00:09:39.614
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:47.910
DAVE RENSIN: Wow, a shy crowd.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:50.280
I do see we have in
the crowd Bart Fromm,

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.260
the mysterious and
amazing Bart Fromm.

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:56.123
Bart is the director of cloud
reliability for all of Google.

00:09:56.123 --> 00:09:58.623
Come on up here, Bart, so we
can embarrass you a little bit.

00:09:58.623 --> 00:10:00.072
BART FROMM: All right.

00:10:00.072 --> 00:10:03.540
So why don't we talk about--

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:09.910
I guess the one I think we
should talk about from a DRT

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:13.270
perspective, is
how large of scale

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:15.720
we try and do the DRT tests out?

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:16.620
Are they small tests?

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:19.240
Are they large tests?

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:21.982
Give people a sense of how
thorough we go into that.

00:10:21.982 --> 00:10:23.440
JOHN WELSH: Yeah,
sure, absolutely.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:28.180
So, thanks, Bart.

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:32.620
So we run tests at a team
level, so individual teams.

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:37.900
But we also run them
across large systems.

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:42.280
So, for example,
we have done tests

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:45.850
where we power cycle a
production data center.

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:49.300
So this is not like just
hitting the Restart button

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:50.980
on the desktop under you.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:52.750
There's lots of
playing that goes

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:56.590
into it, many, many complex
systems that we have to plan,

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:57.400
mitigations.

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:03.130
And try to understand the best
way that we expect it to fail

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:04.439
and be prepared.

00:11:04.439 --> 00:11:06.730
And so, of course, you can
imagine, all these systems--

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:08.860
services are running
within the data center.

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:11.170
We take a lot of
precautions to make sure

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:13.030
that they're out of the
way and to make sure

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:17.110
that SRE can rebalance
that load of those services

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:20.560
across the other data centers.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:23.230
At scale, I think that's one
of the largest, is shutting off

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:25.960
an entire data center and
turning it back on again.

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:27.925
That's pretty large.

00:11:27.925 --> 00:11:29.350
BART FROMM: Steve,
we were earlier

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:32.740
talking about problems
we run into with fiber.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:35.080
Can you talk about
the redundancy

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:37.960
that we build into each
building or into each campus,

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:42.520
so that we're not stuck when
a shark or a boat's anchor

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:44.462
rips through one
of our fiber lines?

00:11:44.462 --> 00:11:45.420
STEVE CARSTENSEN: Sure.

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:52.120
The way we design our network
involves multiple paths

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:54.100
between all of our
points of presence

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:56.390
and our [INAUDIBLE]
data centers.

00:11:56.390 --> 00:11:58.150
So a single fiber
cut should never

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:02.230
cause us a problem, although,
of course, there are exceptions.

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:05.150
We provision the way--

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:09.640
the networking is
sized to be resilient

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.680
in the face of any individual
component going offline,

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:16.480
either within the data
center or outside.

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:18.910
This means that most of the
time when we have fiber cuts--

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:20.830
and we have fiber
cuts all the time--

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:22.950
no one notices.

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:26.500
Most of the time when there is
a customer-visible or even just

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:28.510
Google service-visible
outage, it's

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:32.410
a result of five or six or
seven or 10 concurrent events.

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:36.640
Some fiber cut, some router
down, some other issues.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:38.410
And even in those
cases, we've usually

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:40.900
been able to recover
service, in advance

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:43.900
of the systems that
broke being put back

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:47.620
into service, by routing
traffic better or in a more

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:49.185
intelligent fashion.

00:12:49.185 --> 00:12:50.560
BART FROMM: Sorry,
I'm being told

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:54.400
to move away from the speaker
because I'm creating feedback.

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:58.990
One last question around
the network pieces.

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:01.150
Obviously, we're a
very large network.

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:04.310
One of the things Eric's
talked about was the size.

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:07.736
Can you talk about how
much we see with regards

00:13:07.736 --> 00:13:08.860
to when we get DDoS attack?

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:11.530
Like, how big of an issue
it is that we feel like we

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:13.740
get versus what actually
ends up happening.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:21.810
STEVE CARSTENSEN: I can say
that, in the recent past,

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:25.890
the majority of botnets
that we are aware of

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:30.000
do not cause Google
services problems.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:31.860
We are pretty sure
that there are

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:34.800
larger botnets in the world
that have not attacked us,

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:37.410
so I don't want to say
we are immune, because we

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:38.980
are definitely not immune.

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:41.700
But as far as we can tell, DDoS
attacks we see in the wild,

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:44.790
either directed to us or
that we see backscatter from,

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:46.740
we believe we would
be able to cope with.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:48.989
And certainly the ones that
actually have attacked us,

00:13:48.989 --> 00:13:52.410
we've been able to
cope with quite well.

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:54.870
DAVE RENSIN: All right, let
me shift here for a quick sec.

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:58.020
George, you do a ton
of reviews with teams.

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:00.110
You do a ton of interviewing.

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:03.800
In all the time that you've
looked at stuff, if there

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:07.510
was just one thing out of the
canon of reliability principles

00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:13.520
that you wished everybody
did, what is it?

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:16.110
GEORGE SADLIER: Can we let Steve
tell my favorite story first?

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:17.660
DAVE RENSIN: OK, tell
George's favorite story.

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:18.743
I don't know what that is.

00:14:18.743 --> 00:14:20.410
What is that, Steve?

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:22.070
STEVE CARSTENSEN:
All right, so this

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:27.770
is a story about a major
outage we had a few years ago.

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:29.120
It was actually not an outage.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:31.620
It's one of my favorite sort
of outages, it was a near miss.

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:38.150
Just a bit of background about
the Google infrastructure.

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:41.030
Most of our computers
are in data centers,

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:45.350
which are typically pretty
far from where our users are.

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:48.260
In order to improve the
experience of Google customers,

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:52.220
we run a large fleet
of dedicated servers

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:55.750
on the edge of our network
that do load balancing, TCP

00:14:55.750 --> 00:14:57.500
determination, SSL
termination, et cetera.

00:14:57.500 --> 00:14:59.240
This is also within
Google's network,

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:00.739
all within Google's
control, but out

00:15:00.739 --> 00:15:04.560
sort of in our points appearing
at the edge of the network.

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:08.630
So these edge nodes are
different from our normal data

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:09.130
centers.

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:10.850
So they have their
own set of automation,

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:13.110
turn up, turn down, et cetera.

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:15.170
And a couple of years
ago, one of the engineers

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:17.360
who was working on the turn
up, turn down automation

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:19.250
specifically for
these edge nodes

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:22.370
was exercising a new
pathway, a new particular way

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:23.930
of doing something.

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:26.450
And he was mistakenly
under the impression

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:28.432
that the turn down
automation was idempotent.

00:15:28.432 --> 00:15:30.390
That is, if you ran it
twice on the same thing,

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:31.760
nothing new would happen.

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:34.050
Unfortunately, he was wrong.

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:35.690
So first, he ran
at once, testing

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:38.630
on a particular workflow,
a particular step that

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:40.430
sends all the machines
in a particular edge

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:42.260
node to something we
call disk erase, which

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:46.610
is when we erase all the disks
as part of doing turn down.

00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:47.950
And that worked just fine.

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:51.374
It was a satellite that was
already going out of service.

00:15:51.374 --> 00:15:52.790
There was a bug
with the workflow.

00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:55.160
So we ran it again on
the same satellite.

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:58.400
Unfortunately, the way that this
actually worked is, you say,

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:01.220
I want to run this thing
on this set of machines.

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:04.040
And the way you get
this set of machines,

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:06.860
very briefly, is
you basically say,

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:09.860
I want all of the machines that
fit this particular pattern.

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:14.240
Unfortunately, the result of
"no machines match this pattern"

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:18.020
is not "no machines,"
it's "all machines."

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:20.120
We have seen this
failure many times

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:23.210
in very, very
different scenarios,

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:26.726
but anytime you interpret
an input as everything,

00:16:26.726 --> 00:16:28.100
you should really
think carefully

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:29.120
about what you're doing.

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:30.537
Because of the
layers here, no one

00:16:30.537 --> 00:16:32.120
really understood
that this is the way

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:33.680
that this would be interpreted.

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:38.150
The upshot was this engineer
accidentally called disk erase

00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:41.650
on every edge node in the
fleet at the same time.

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:44.870
Another funny, to
me, side note, is

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:47.927
my team, who runs the edge
nodes, didn't at that time

00:16:47.927 --> 00:16:49.760
believe disk erase
worked on the edge nodes.

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:52.734
Like, we just thought
that it should work,

00:16:52.734 --> 00:16:53.900
we hadn't gotten it to work.

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:56.690
We thought, it probably
just wouldn't do anything.

00:16:56.690 --> 00:16:59.870
It turns out, we were
horribly, hilariously wrong.

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:01.460
In the course of
about 20 minutes,

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:06.140
every edge node in the
fleet lost its mind,

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:11.030
lost all of its configs and
binaries, and stopped serving.

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:14.510
Now, you didn't hear about
this because the way we design,

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:17.589
the way we do front-end
serving, involves

00:17:17.589 --> 00:17:19.579
as seamless failover as we can.

00:17:19.579 --> 00:17:21.140
TCP connections
were reset, so there

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:24.650
was some spike of new
connections being formed,

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:27.004
but all of the traffic,
relatively seamlessly,

00:17:27.004 --> 00:17:28.670
failed over to the
back-end data centers

00:17:28.670 --> 00:17:31.434
where we have reserve capacity.

00:17:31.434 --> 00:17:33.350
There was also some
congestion on our backbone

00:17:33.350 --> 00:17:34.682
and with our peers.

00:17:34.682 --> 00:17:36.140
Because of the
details of how we do

00:17:36.140 --> 00:17:39.080
BGP, when we terminate
TCP connections in data

00:17:39.080 --> 00:17:41.000
centers versus
our edge nodes, it

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:43.144
changes where the bits
egress from our network.

00:17:43.144 --> 00:17:44.810
And so some of our
peers were congested,

00:17:44.810 --> 00:17:48.560
and we had to do various traffic
engineering on our backbone

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:49.730
in order to resolve that.

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:51.940
But for the most
part, no one noticed.

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:55.570
And no one noticed over
the next 48 to 72 hours

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:57.710
as my team put together--

00:17:57.710 --> 00:17:59.300
basically everyone on the team--

00:17:59.300 --> 00:18:02.780
into two 12-hour
shifts in the Bay Area

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:07.190
and in London, trying very,
very hard to put the edge back

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:09.230
into place as fast as we could.

00:18:09.230 --> 00:18:12.510
We ran into all sorts
of problems doing this.

00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:16.400
But again-- and I can talk about
this if somebody wanted me to--

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:20.570
the upshot is, defense in
depth is a very useful thing.

00:18:20.570 --> 00:18:21.920
No one noticed.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:24.704
We were really lucky
that we found this out

00:18:24.704 --> 00:18:25.870
in this particular scenario.

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:29.030
It turns out this exact
piece of automation

00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:31.190
could have done the same
thing to a different class

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:35.300
of machines that would have
been far far, far worse.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:39.530
So, in a way, we're really
lucky we did it to the edge.

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:44.690
And we had a tremendously
awesome postmortem internally.

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:46.114
It was a great
bonding experience,

00:18:46.114 --> 00:18:47.030
I think, for the team.

00:18:47.030 --> 00:18:49.340
And it still is a story
that we talk about,

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:52.960
because it's a very formative
outage, well, near miss.

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:55.790
DAVE RENSIN: And there are
times where defense in depth,

00:18:55.790 --> 00:18:58.760
in a weird way, is not
your friend, unless it's

00:18:58.760 --> 00:18:59.770
accompanied by testing.

00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:02.600
And I recall we had
an incident where--

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:05.030
we've talked about this
publicly-- where we pushed

00:19:05.030 --> 00:19:07.220
a bad route, that BGP route.

00:19:07.220 --> 00:19:09.710
And, because there
was so much capacity

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:12.350
for network in so many
different places globally,

00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:14.370
what happened was is,
if you can imagine

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:17.130
a map of all these connections
of where packets could

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:19.230
come in and go out
of our network,

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:20.670
as the route was
propagating they

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:24.060
were just going dark
one after the other.

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:26.400
But it took us a while
to notice because we

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:29.160
have fiber connecting all these
things, and it's very fast,

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:31.950
and there's so much capacity
that the traffic was just

00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:35.189
being moved to other
parts of the network.

00:19:35.189 --> 00:19:36.730
And you didn't see
it in the latency.

00:19:36.730 --> 00:19:38.396
You didn't see it in
the characteristics

00:19:38.396 --> 00:19:39.220
you would expect.

00:19:39.220 --> 00:19:41.940
And we kind of didn't
really feel the effect

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:44.190
until essentially-- it's a
little bit of hyperbole--

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:46.470
the last node went out.

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:50.310
Like, the last exit to the door
closed, and then the whole flow

00:19:50.310 --> 00:19:52.910
stopped for a few minutes.

00:19:52.910 --> 00:19:54.895
That was the case,
I think, where

00:19:54.895 --> 00:19:56.520
the redundancy and
the defense in depth

00:19:56.520 --> 00:19:59.610
was good, but not necessarily
accompanied by the right kind

00:19:59.610 --> 00:20:00.720
of monitoring.

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:02.180
STEVE CARSTENSEN: Absolutely.

00:20:02.180 --> 00:20:06.600
And we've had variations of that
outage at other times as well.

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:09.542
I mean, the lesson is,
defense in depth is great,

00:20:09.542 --> 00:20:11.250
but you want to know
when you start using

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:12.760
other layers of your defense.

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:14.134
And that was our
problem here, is

00:20:14.134 --> 00:20:17.130
that the particular monitoring
in the scenario that Dave

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:21.420
is talking about was
not precise enough

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:24.360
to tell us that
individual locations were

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:26.070
no longer attracting traffic.

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:28.080
All we could tell, at
that point in time,

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:30.900
all our monitoring told us was
that traffic was still getting

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:34.780
through to the back ends,
until suddenly it wasn't.

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:37.330
DAVE RENSIN: OK, George,
you demurred to Steve

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:38.580
when I asked you the question.

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:40.074
We're not letting
you off the hook.

00:20:40.074 --> 00:20:41.466
GEORGE SADLIER: Can
we tell more stories?

00:20:41.466 --> 00:20:41.930
DAVE RENSIN: By all means.

00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:42.480
Tell them.

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:43.470
GEORGE SADLIER:
Because we've got time.

00:20:43.470 --> 00:20:44.100
We've got stories.

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:45.391
DAVE RENSIN: Yeah, don't worry.

00:20:45.391 --> 00:20:46.002
Go on.

00:20:46.002 --> 00:20:47.460
GEORGE SADLIER: I
was going to say,

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:50.460
this is a classic
set of failure modes.

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:54.850
We had an interesting outage
in a system we will not name,

00:20:54.850 --> 00:20:59.190
near outage, where
our configuration

00:20:59.190 --> 00:21:00.330
change was rolling out.

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:02.490
Which was at an
upstream system, which

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:05.939
we were mostly not aware of
because we didn't need to know.

00:21:05.939 --> 00:21:08.355
But we we had a low traffic
alarm from one of our clusters

00:21:08.355 --> 00:21:11.130
because we a traffic levels,
and these clusters of pretty

00:21:11.130 --> 00:21:11.796
are pretty busy.

00:21:11.796 --> 00:21:14.010
So when we didn't get any
traffic in the cluster,

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:16.310
that was cause for
an alert to go off.

00:21:16.310 --> 00:21:17.870
And we're, what's going on?

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:18.680
Let's go look.

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:19.692
OK, there's no traffic.

00:21:19.692 --> 00:21:21.150
And as we're
debugging that traffic

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:23.025
to figure out what's
wrong with that cluster,

00:21:23.025 --> 00:21:25.360
another alarm goes off
in another cluster.

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:28.560
And it's like, oh no.

00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:30.060
And now we are
frantically debugging

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:31.870
because, as we're
watching out maps,

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:36.420
we're watching traffic
dropping offline in no order we

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:40.410
can determine, as
traffic vanishes

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:43.110
from front-end serving clusters.

00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:45.097
And at some point,
somebody in the team

00:21:45.097 --> 00:21:46.680
made the connection
that was following

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:50.930
the exact pattern of
the Google front end,

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:54.390
it's one of our
front-end proxy systems

00:21:54.390 --> 00:21:56.220
and it's config [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:56.220 --> 00:22:00.040
Which happens to be
one of Steve's teams.

00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:02.640
So we reached out in
a panic, and begged

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:04.712
them to stop their push
while we figured out

00:22:04.712 --> 00:22:05.920
what on earth had gone wrong.

00:22:05.920 --> 00:22:10.134
And sure enough, somebody
who will not be named--

00:22:10.134 --> 00:22:11.800
STEVE CARSTENSEN:
Blameless postmortems,

00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:12.870
blameless postmortems.

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:13.750
GEORGE SADLIER:
--had made a typo--

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:15.076
DAVE RENSIN: Blaming,
which just identified him.

00:22:15.076 --> 00:22:15.960
GEORGE SADLIER: Yeah.

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:18.160
Had made a typo
in a config change

00:22:18.160 --> 00:22:20.340
and it had been reviewed
by numerous people, none

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:23.070
of whom had spotted the typo.

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:26.280
And so as the conflict went
out, the front end conveniently

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:29.864
forgot the particular
back end in question

00:22:29.864 --> 00:22:31.280
and stopped sending
traffic to it.

00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:35.460
When we halted it
with one cluster left.

00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:36.390
Just one.

00:22:36.390 --> 00:22:38.410
And so we processed
all of our global load

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:41.520
on one cluster for
24 hours before we

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:43.485
could get that corrected.

00:22:43.485 --> 00:22:44.610
This was quite a while ago.

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:46.800
It was a lot harder to
roll back those systems.

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:48.870
These days, we could
roll back very quickly.

00:22:48.870 --> 00:22:52.380
In those days, it was
a bit more of a journey

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:53.650
to achieve rollback.

00:22:53.650 --> 00:22:56.417
And so we decided that
it was actually safer

00:22:56.417 --> 00:22:58.500
to stay in place, because
we were doing all right.

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:00.166
It was running slower
than it needed to,

00:23:00.166 --> 00:23:02.640
but it was
functioning correctly.

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:04.350
So we could hold out
24 hours until we

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:06.510
could get a safe rollback.

00:23:06.510 --> 00:23:09.315
So much fun and games.

00:23:12.480 --> 00:23:14.070
JOHN WELSH: Both
George and Steve, you

00:23:14.070 --> 00:23:17.772
guys both mentioned
postmortem culture.

00:23:17.772 --> 00:23:19.230
Maybe George, you
want to elaborate

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:20.314
on what that means for us?

00:23:20.314 --> 00:23:22.563
GEORGE SADLIER: I'm assuming
everyone's read the book,

00:23:22.563 --> 00:23:24.500
so they know all
about postmortems.

00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:25.660
No, I mean, we really--

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:28.510
this is what we consider one
of the key principles of Cyber

00:23:28.510 --> 00:23:31.800
Reliability Engineering and just
good reliability engineering

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:32.850
in general.

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:35.010
The idea that, if
you're moving quickly,

00:23:35.010 --> 00:23:37.069
you're working on big,
complicated systems.

00:23:37.069 --> 00:23:38.610
These large-scale
distributed systems

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:40.272
have emergent properties.

00:23:40.272 --> 00:23:42.480
Things are going to happen
that you couldn't predict.

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:43.688
Things are going to go wrong.

00:23:43.688 --> 00:23:45.270
People are going
to make mistakes.

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:47.340
And the ability
and the willingness

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:49.590
to go back and
thoroughly understand

00:23:49.590 --> 00:23:54.180
what happened so you can
determine how to prevent

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:56.550
it happening again, and
learn from the outage,

00:23:56.550 --> 00:23:57.990
is super important.

00:23:57.990 --> 00:24:02.550
But the only way you can have
a culture that really does that

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:05.040
is by endorsing
that blameless part.

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:05.940
Right?

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:07.770
Humans make mistakes.

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:09.000
It's what we do.

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:11.667
Humans are excellent
at making mistakes.

00:24:11.667 --> 00:24:13.500
If you want something
to not make a mistake,

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:14.450
you use silicon.

00:24:14.450 --> 00:24:16.910
The problem is, the silicon
is programmed by humans.

00:24:16.910 --> 00:24:18.451
And what did I just
say about humans?

00:24:20.780 --> 00:24:24.860
So we have to be willing
to go in with no blame.

00:24:24.860 --> 00:24:25.790
It's going to happen.

00:24:25.790 --> 00:24:27.450
We're going to introduce bugs.

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:28.820
Software engineers write bugs.

00:24:28.820 --> 00:24:30.830
We're quite good at that.

00:24:30.830 --> 00:24:33.680
System's going to fail
in unexpected ways.

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:40.219
If we were expecting humans to
never make mistakes, you fail.

00:24:40.219 --> 00:24:41.510
Human's going to make mistakes.

00:24:41.510 --> 00:24:42.980
If you've got a process
that requires a human

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:45.560
not to make a mistake, you're
going to have an outage.

00:24:45.560 --> 00:24:47.870
And so, sitting back,
and looking at that,

00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:50.875
and understanding that
is really important.

00:24:50.875 --> 00:24:53.000
And so you've got to have
the blameless postmortem,

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:55.532
the willingness to go
back and look at it.

00:24:55.532 --> 00:24:56.990
STEVE CARSTENSEN:
And on that note,

00:24:56.990 --> 00:24:58.890
one of the most
important things,

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:01.910
in my opinion, at least, about
a blameless postmortem culture

00:25:01.910 --> 00:25:05.870
is not blaming the human
even in the actions.

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:08.810
So when you're thinking about,
how do I avoid this problem?

00:25:08.810 --> 00:25:11.000
It is a mistake if
you say, the way

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:12.890
I will avoid this problem
is by just training

00:25:12.890 --> 00:25:15.230
the humans better, or by
teaching the humans to do

00:25:15.230 --> 00:25:18.740
something more intelligent,
or by updating the procedure

00:25:18.740 --> 00:25:20.284
that the human uses.

00:25:20.284 --> 00:25:21.950
Maybe you should do
all of those things,

00:25:21.950 --> 00:25:23.510
but that's not
actually a root cause.

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:25.780
The way you should
look at an outage,

00:25:25.780 --> 00:25:27.740
and the postmortem
about an outage,

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:30.560
is identify why the
system allowed the human

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:32.210
to make the mistake.

00:25:32.210 --> 00:25:34.876
You should fix the system
so the human cannot do that.

00:25:34.876 --> 00:25:35.802
GEORGE SADLIER: Yeah.

00:25:35.802 --> 00:25:37.510
I think there's a
classic example of this

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:41.180
from quite some time ago now.

00:25:41.180 --> 00:25:46.670
There was an outage
in a web mail system

00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:50.840
where, ultimately, the trigger
for the incident was a human.

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:54.020
A human pushed a change.

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:57.470
And the human in question--
he was a friend of mine--

00:25:57.470 --> 00:25:58.530
was very upset.

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:00.110
He had felt that
he had personally

00:26:00.110 --> 00:26:03.680
caused an outage, a major outage
that basically all of Google's

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:06.065
users had experienced.

00:26:06.065 --> 00:26:10.040
But the reality is, is he
was working through a change

00:26:10.040 --> 00:26:11.270
process to change--

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:13.400
it was actually a contact
management system--

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:15.740
to change the way the
available back ends

00:26:15.740 --> 00:26:18.260
for the contact
management system

00:26:18.260 --> 00:26:21.027
and how they were served.

00:26:21.027 --> 00:26:23.110
And this system had gotten
a lot more complicated.

00:26:23.110 --> 00:26:26.400
I'm at system
scale very quickly.

00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:28.160
And so a process that
kind of made sense

00:26:28.160 --> 00:26:29.300
when there were
two or three nodes

00:26:29.300 --> 00:26:31.030
didn't really make
nearly as much sense

00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:32.750
when we would go to a 300.

00:26:32.750 --> 00:26:34.280
And so the process
required a human

00:26:34.280 --> 00:26:39.440
to review a machine-readable
dataset and the diffs on it

00:26:39.440 --> 00:26:41.570
and make sure that
it was correct.

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:43.330
That was the control.

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:47.660
Human should review
diff and approve it.

00:26:47.660 --> 00:26:49.520
This happens a couple
of times a week.

00:26:49.520 --> 00:26:50.360
And you know what?

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:52.400
99.9% of the time
there's nothing there.

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:54.740
So of course, the human looks
at it, looks about right,

00:26:54.740 --> 00:26:56.014
moves on.

00:26:56.014 --> 00:26:57.680
And we can sit back
and we can say, hey,

00:26:57.680 --> 00:26:59.360
they should have
been more careful.

00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:01.040
But that's ridiculous, right?

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:04.140
It's the classic thing of,
like, 90% of the job is boring

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:06.380
and one second of
absolute terror.

00:27:06.380 --> 00:27:09.320
It's when the thing
is almost always

00:27:09.320 --> 00:27:13.505
right except when it's not,
humans are a terrible choice,

00:27:13.505 --> 00:27:14.300
right?

00:27:14.300 --> 00:27:16.880
Human mental pattern
matching does not

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:18.780
function in that environment.

00:27:18.780 --> 00:27:20.747
So the engineer did what
every other engineer

00:27:20.747 --> 00:27:23.330
in their position would do, and
they looked at the diff, looks

00:27:23.330 --> 00:27:26.840
about right, off it went.

00:27:26.840 --> 00:27:30.260
And left that system with
insufficient serving capacity,

00:27:30.260 --> 00:27:32.990
which triggered a cascading
failure of that contact

00:27:32.990 --> 00:27:34.970
management system,
which, in turn, caused

00:27:34.970 --> 00:27:37.940
severe consequences
further up the stack.

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:39.131
There's many problems here.

00:27:39.131 --> 00:27:40.880
A failure in the contact
management system

00:27:40.880 --> 00:27:44.420
shouldn't prevent
access to your email.

00:27:44.420 --> 00:27:48.010
But that process had not
scaled with the system.

00:27:48.010 --> 00:27:50.630
And the idea that somehow
this engineer was at fault

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:51.980
is ridiculous.

00:27:51.980 --> 00:27:54.590
That outage was
guaranteed the moment

00:27:54.590 --> 00:27:56.694
we put that process in place.

00:27:56.694 --> 00:27:58.610
All right, the fault
there was not the humans.

00:27:58.610 --> 00:28:02.510
Didn't matter what human was
at the keyboard that day.

00:28:02.510 --> 00:28:05.060
If it didn't happen that day,
it would happen the day after.

00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:06.800
It could have happened
the month before.

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:08.750
That outage was inevitable.

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:09.605
It's not the human.

00:28:09.605 --> 00:28:12.399
Human just happened to be
the person on the keyboard.

00:28:12.399 --> 00:28:14.690
And so another example is
really important to remember,

00:28:14.690 --> 00:28:17.360
we were asking a
human to do something

00:28:17.360 --> 00:28:19.550
that humans are not good at.

00:28:19.550 --> 00:28:21.800
The failure was in the
process and the technology.

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:23.777
There should have been a--

00:28:23.777 --> 00:28:25.610
the machines should
have been checking that.

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:28.850
The machines should have been
doing velocity checking, which

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:30.980
is basically a
technique we're saying,

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:34.010
this is the expected size of any
change in this configuration.

00:28:34.010 --> 00:28:37.184
If it's larger than this, stop.

00:28:37.184 --> 00:28:38.100
But we didn't do that.

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:44.650
JOHN WELSH: So you
touched on something

00:28:44.650 --> 00:28:48.770
about looking at the
assumptions of the system.

00:28:48.770 --> 00:28:51.284
And so that's one
of the main things

00:28:51.284 --> 00:28:53.200
we do when we do disaster
testing is challenge

00:28:53.200 --> 00:28:54.490
assumptions about systems.

00:28:54.490 --> 00:28:57.610
Especially long-held
assumptions about systems.

00:28:57.610 --> 00:29:00.130
So maybe you have a backup.

00:29:00.130 --> 00:29:02.170
Well, we always
back our data up.

00:29:02.170 --> 00:29:03.070
Of course it's good.

00:29:03.070 --> 00:29:04.300
Of course we can restore it.

00:29:04.300 --> 00:29:06.257
Why would there
ever be a problem?

00:29:06.257 --> 00:29:07.840
So I would come along
and I would say,

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:10.480
we really should test
that backup and restore.

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:12.730
And we should look at the
integrity of that data

00:29:12.730 --> 00:29:16.470
because your data isn't actually
useful unless you can use it.

00:29:16.470 --> 00:29:16.970
Right?

00:29:16.970 --> 00:29:19.390
So having a backup
is just one thing.

00:29:19.390 --> 00:29:22.270
Being able to restore it
and use it is another thing.

00:29:22.270 --> 00:29:23.890
And maybe failing
over it, right?

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:27.700
I have, oh, I'm in multiple
zones, multiple regions.

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:30.340
Well, actually go and
test your systems,

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:34.240
and drain a zone, or whatnot,
so that you can actually

00:29:34.240 --> 00:29:39.640
validate practically that these
assumptions work correctly

00:29:39.640 --> 00:29:43.600
in different failure modes
and different scenarios.

00:29:43.600 --> 00:29:45.220
DAVE RENSIN: I'm
newer to Google.

00:29:45.220 --> 00:29:47.095
I haven't been here as
long as you guys have.

00:29:47.095 --> 00:29:49.120
And I remember, not
long after I joined,

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:53.000
someone was explaining to be
blameless postmortem culture.

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:54.132
And I was skeptical.

00:29:54.132 --> 00:29:56.590
Because I've worked at other
companies, and the first thing

00:29:56.590 --> 00:29:58.336
you do is you blame someone.

00:29:58.336 --> 00:30:00.460
And I remember I had this
conversation with George.

00:30:00.460 --> 00:30:02.310
I was in Seattle.

00:30:02.310 --> 00:30:04.510
And I said, you know,
that sounds good,

00:30:04.510 --> 00:30:07.160
but I don't believe you.

00:30:07.160 --> 00:30:08.312
And he said, oh, really?

00:30:08.312 --> 00:30:10.520
And I don't remember who
this was, and even if I did,

00:30:10.520 --> 00:30:11.200
I wouldn't use their name.

00:30:11.200 --> 00:30:12.074
He's like, come here.

00:30:12.074 --> 00:30:13.750
And he walked me
over to someone,

00:30:13.750 --> 00:30:18.740
and he said, hi, this is Dave.

00:30:18.740 --> 00:30:22.320
Tell Dave, when were
you last promoted?

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:24.580
And the person told him,
it's been six months ago.

00:30:24.580 --> 00:30:27.860
And he said, why'd
you get promoted?

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:30.514
He said, well, I
broke something.

00:30:30.514 --> 00:30:31.420
All right, what?

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:33.711
That seems the opposite of
the way that normally works.

00:30:33.711 --> 00:30:36.160
Please use more words.

00:30:36.160 --> 00:30:38.140
And he said, oh, well,
what happened was is,

00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:41.410
I was doing a thing,
and I made a mistake.

00:30:41.410 --> 00:30:43.690
I made a mistake and I broke it.

00:30:43.690 --> 00:30:45.020
And that was bad.

00:30:45.020 --> 00:30:47.600
And then, so then of
course, I fixed it.

00:30:47.600 --> 00:30:50.140
And then I wrote the postmortem.

00:30:50.140 --> 00:30:53.560
And then I built software
so the system wouldn't

00:30:53.560 --> 00:30:56.530
allow the next unlucky
person, who happened to be me,

00:30:56.530 --> 00:30:58.309
to break it the same way again.

00:30:58.309 --> 00:31:00.850
And it turned out to be a bunch
more work than people thought

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:02.125
it would be, because it was a
complicated thing because it

00:31:02.125 --> 00:31:04.150
was a big emergent system.

00:31:04.150 --> 00:31:07.060
And lo and behold, the
company was like, great,

00:31:07.060 --> 00:31:09.074
that is working as intended.

00:31:09.074 --> 00:31:10.990
You just happened to be
the unlucky person who

00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:13.480
made the mistake that someone
was eventually going to make,

00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:16.210
you raised your hand, you
fixed it, you led the process,

00:31:16.210 --> 00:31:17.440
you built the software.

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:20.560
Now another person can't
make that mistake again.

00:31:20.560 --> 00:31:21.660
You're promoted.

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:24.310
And I just remember, that was
the thing that really stuck

00:31:24.310 --> 00:31:26.808
with me, was that conversation.

00:31:30.150 --> 00:31:31.650
GEORGE SADLIER: I
was actually going

00:31:31.650 --> 00:31:35.860
to come back, because I think
a point John just made about--

00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:39.300
there's an old adage in
systems administration.

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:41.780
Your backups are only as
good as your restores.

00:31:41.780 --> 00:31:44.500
And one of the things that came
out of one of John's DRT tests

00:31:44.500 --> 00:31:48.232
on one of my systems is, we'd
done a bunch restore testing.

00:31:48.232 --> 00:31:49.440
And we knew we could restore.

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:50.310
Restores were great.

00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:52.890
They work fantastically,
they're pretty quick.

00:31:52.890 --> 00:31:55.140
And then the DRT team showed
up and said, OK, well, we

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:56.514
know you can do
account restores.

00:31:56.514 --> 00:31:57.990
Let's restore the entire system.

00:31:57.990 --> 00:31:59.940
And we're like,
sure, no problem.

00:31:59.940 --> 00:32:01.710
Get the thing kicked
off, get it running,

00:32:01.710 --> 00:32:02.709
it's running a bit slow.

00:32:02.709 --> 00:32:05.490
Estimated completion date--

00:32:05.490 --> 00:32:07.830
2119.

00:32:07.830 --> 00:32:10.550
Not the time, the year.

00:32:10.550 --> 00:32:13.726
And it was like, oh, yeah,
each individual restore was

00:32:13.726 --> 00:32:15.600
pretty quick, but we
hadn't actually sat down

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:17.104
and run the numbers.

00:32:17.104 --> 00:32:19.770
And it turns out, because of the
way the storage system worked--

00:32:19.770 --> 00:32:23.040
it was a random access
and some other things--

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:26.940
the time between account
restores was pretty long,

00:32:26.940 --> 00:32:29.750
and the result was
we'd never tried to--

00:32:29.750 --> 00:32:32.430
we'd done individual restores
and we'd done batch restores,

00:32:32.430 --> 00:32:35.160
but we'd never tried to
restore a really big group.

00:32:35.160 --> 00:32:37.590
And that was the only time
we realized that, actually,

00:32:37.590 --> 00:32:39.840
if we ever needed to
do a complete restore,

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:42.120
we were not going be able to.

00:32:42.120 --> 00:32:44.207
And that was a result
of one of the folks

00:32:44.207 --> 00:32:46.290
that John works with coming
to us and just saying,

00:32:46.290 --> 00:32:47.190
can you do this?

00:32:47.190 --> 00:32:49.730
And us saying, of course we can.

00:32:49.730 --> 00:32:50.320
We'll test it.

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:51.070
OK, we'll test it.

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:53.130
It'll be fun.

00:32:53.130 --> 00:32:56.760
So anyway, short story
is, we promptly rewrote

00:32:56.760 --> 00:33:00.360
our restore system to do a lot
of our restores in parallel.

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:02.860
Strangely enough, made
a huge difference.

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:06.199
And now it restores much,
much, much, much, much faster.

00:33:06.199 --> 00:33:07.740
But I still remember
the email coming

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:10.200
across saying, yeah, no, we'll
be done in about 200 years.

00:33:14.720 --> 00:33:17.780
I was going to say, there must
be some other funny stories

00:33:17.780 --> 00:33:19.659
have come out of DRT tests.

00:33:19.659 --> 00:33:22.200
DAVE RENSIN: That was the other
thing I was going to ask you,

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:22.850
John.

00:33:22.850 --> 00:33:26.730
So you sit in a
privileged position.

00:33:26.730 --> 00:33:28.730
Not only to see wacky
things, but to cause them,

00:33:28.730 --> 00:33:30.810
to make them happen.

00:33:30.810 --> 00:33:32.780
So I'll give you a
choice, fielder's choice,

00:33:32.780 --> 00:33:34.190
and then we're going to
play a game of topper.

00:33:34.190 --> 00:33:36.023
But I'll tell you,
explain that in a second.

00:33:36.023 --> 00:33:39.230
So what is either
the craziest thing

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:43.137
you caused, either on purpose
or not on purpose, or just

00:33:43.137 --> 00:33:45.470
the craziest thing you have
seen as a part of doing DRT?

00:33:45.470 --> 00:33:47.092
I want to hear this.

00:33:47.092 --> 00:33:48.800
JOHN WELSH: That I
can talk about, right?

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:49.310
DAVE RENSIN: Yeah.

00:33:49.310 --> 00:33:50.990
Yeah, right, keeping in mind,
I'm sorry, keeping in mind

00:33:50.990 --> 00:33:53.531
we're in the middle of Google's
semiannual performance review

00:33:53.531 --> 00:33:54.090
season.

00:33:54.090 --> 00:33:56.381
So you just want to hold that
in the back of your head.

00:33:56.381 --> 00:33:58.630
JOHN WELSH: Right,
right, of course, yeah.

00:33:58.630 --> 00:34:04.070
Yeah, so I think
probably a good story is,

00:34:04.070 --> 00:34:07.280
so I mentioned earlier that we
have a company-wide disaster

00:34:07.280 --> 00:34:09.260
recovery test week event.

00:34:09.260 --> 00:34:12.989
So we're running several
hundred tests over a week.

00:34:12.989 --> 00:34:16.639
A lot of them are very
disruptive to ourselves, not

00:34:16.639 --> 00:34:19.370
to our customers, ideally.

00:34:19.370 --> 00:34:23.870
And this one particular
cycle we got about halfway

00:34:23.870 --> 00:34:26.699
through the week and
we're planning scenarios.

00:34:26.699 --> 00:34:28.699
We have kind of a story
that goes along with it.

00:34:28.699 --> 00:34:31.909
Like, maybe an asteroid
is hitting the Bay Area,

00:34:31.909 --> 00:34:36.020
or maybe some storm is
affecting the East Coast.

00:34:36.020 --> 00:34:38.270
So it turns out about
halfway through the week,

00:34:38.270 --> 00:34:41.840
a large winter storm actually
did occur and impacted

00:34:41.840 --> 00:34:46.750
the southeast US,
which wasn't great.

00:34:46.750 --> 00:34:49.850
Some places had reduced
staffing, things like that.

00:34:49.850 --> 00:34:51.860
On top of that,
on the West Coast,

00:34:51.860 --> 00:34:55.040
we had a real fiber
cut that coincided.

00:34:55.040 --> 00:34:56.330
And so that was OK.

00:34:56.330 --> 00:34:58.700
That's fine, one fiber
cut, no big deal.

00:34:58.700 --> 00:35:01.400
But then, on the East Coast, in
a totally separate data center,

00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:04.660
we had a network
line card failure.

00:35:04.660 --> 00:35:07.850
And OK, fine, a little
bit of additional latency.

00:35:07.850 --> 00:35:09.340
We're still OK.

00:35:09.340 --> 00:35:12.470
Let's get a card shipped
out there and replaced.

00:35:12.470 --> 00:35:14.900
That card was dead on arrival.

00:35:14.900 --> 00:35:16.630
Now there's this winter storm.

00:35:16.630 --> 00:35:19.440
It now is going to take
FedEx or UPS or whatever

00:35:19.440 --> 00:35:23.000
it was an additional two
days to get a line card.

00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:25.940
So now, we're going to
need to recalibrate and say

00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:29.730
we're going to run with a
bit of additional latency

00:35:29.730 --> 00:35:33.060
for longer than maybe we would
have liked, but that's OK.

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:35.750
We're still working on it,
working through things.

00:35:35.750 --> 00:35:38.120
All right, we're still
running our tests.

00:35:38.120 --> 00:35:44.120
We were running a test of one of
our storage layers in one area.

00:35:44.120 --> 00:35:48.350
And nearby, there was an
unexpected power event

00:35:48.350 --> 00:35:51.560
that affected the storage
layer that we were testing

00:35:51.560 --> 00:35:53.420
in a different
location that reduced

00:35:53.420 --> 00:35:55.040
their available capacity.

00:35:55.040 --> 00:35:58.340
So we're kind of like piling on
with all these real situations.

00:35:58.340 --> 00:36:01.370
So I think the thing
that surprised me most

00:36:01.370 --> 00:36:03.800
was that here we are
planning these disasters

00:36:03.800 --> 00:36:05.330
and trying to do
disaster testing,

00:36:05.330 --> 00:36:08.750
and all these real events are
occurring at the same time.

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:12.020
Sort of, if you think it, it
will happen kind of problem.

00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:15.230
So we paused our tests, we
rolled back certain tests.

00:36:15.230 --> 00:36:18.150
And we totally changed the
dynamic of our war room.

00:36:18.150 --> 00:36:20.000
So we took our team of
experts, and instead

00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:22.250
of trying to cause
issues, we started

00:36:22.250 --> 00:36:24.080
to help resolve the issues.

00:36:24.080 --> 00:36:26.060
So we helped with
incident management.

00:36:26.060 --> 00:36:28.670
We helped the teams
that were directly

00:36:28.670 --> 00:36:30.440
trying to address
some of these issues.

00:36:30.440 --> 00:36:31.640
We could do troubleshooting.

00:36:31.640 --> 00:36:34.490
We could actually fix some of
the issues for some of them

00:36:34.490 --> 00:36:37.940
in some cases and help get
everything back together.

00:36:37.940 --> 00:36:41.330
So I think what surprised
me is the way that--

00:36:41.330 --> 00:36:45.750
don't panic when disaster
occurs, because it will occur.

00:36:45.750 --> 00:36:48.900
Keep a cool head, invoke your
incident management protocols,

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:53.210
lend a hand, and a were very
creative and very supportive

00:36:53.210 --> 00:36:54.950
in helping get
everything back up

00:36:54.950 --> 00:36:57.740
and running very quickly
and very successfully.

00:36:57.740 --> 00:37:00.890
So as someone who is
trying to cause problems

00:37:00.890 --> 00:37:02.570
in a constructive
way, it was very

00:37:02.570 --> 00:37:06.170
cool and surprising to see
us actually pull together

00:37:06.170 --> 00:37:08.290
and fix things in a big way.

00:37:08.290 --> 00:37:10.040
GEORGE SADLIER: And
being on the receiving

00:37:10.040 --> 00:37:11.750
end of a lot of that
testing, I remember

00:37:11.750 --> 00:37:13.025
a couple of
brainstorming sessions

00:37:13.025 --> 00:37:14.810
as we're getting ready
for our annual testing.

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:15.890
And we're sitting
there saying, OK,

00:37:15.890 --> 00:37:18.120
well, let's work through
our emergency procedures

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:19.520
and figure out what we
want to test this year.

00:37:19.520 --> 00:37:20.810
And I said, well,
we'll test that one.

00:37:20.810 --> 00:37:23.540
Oh, yeah, we actually did that
a month ago because it happened.

00:37:23.540 --> 00:37:24.550
Oh, yeah.

00:37:24.550 --> 00:37:26.910
Oh, no, we did that
one recently, too.

00:37:26.910 --> 00:37:28.390
Oh.

00:37:28.390 --> 00:37:31.040
Actually we've tested everything
so far in the last six months

00:37:31.040 --> 00:37:33.332
because everything has broken
at some point or another.

00:37:33.332 --> 00:37:35.956
And we had to come up with some
particularly creative and crazy

00:37:35.956 --> 00:37:37.470
tests because it's
like, yeah, no.

00:37:37.470 --> 00:37:41.170
I mean, it turns out there's
two types of emergencies.

00:37:41.170 --> 00:37:44.060
There's routine emergencies
and emergent emergencies.

00:37:44.060 --> 00:37:47.482
And we're pretty good
at routine emergencies.

00:37:47.482 --> 00:37:49.940
JOHN WELSH: I mean, I think
you raise an interesting point,

00:37:49.940 --> 00:37:54.110
too, which is that we like
to look at the systems,

00:37:54.110 --> 00:37:57.942
and test the systems, and try
to make them fall tolerant,

00:37:57.942 --> 00:37:59.900
but there's also humans
involved, right, as you

00:37:59.900 --> 00:38:01.025
were talking about earlier.

00:38:01.025 --> 00:38:04.070
So practicing incident
management is really important.

00:38:04.070 --> 00:38:07.490
Being able to
quickly invoke people

00:38:07.490 --> 00:38:09.790
to respond to the incident.

00:38:09.790 --> 00:38:14.010
That they have muscle
memory there is a big deal.

00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:17.950
A lot of times at Google we
have a very horizontal structure

00:38:17.950 --> 00:38:19.643
with people and management.

00:38:19.643 --> 00:38:23.534
So when we have an
incident, we tend

00:38:23.534 --> 00:38:24.950
to invoke this
incident management

00:38:24.950 --> 00:38:27.160
protocol that's
more hierarchical,

00:38:27.160 --> 00:38:29.870
because the scenario
calls for it.

00:38:29.870 --> 00:38:31.759
You need someone who
can call the shots.

00:38:31.759 --> 00:38:33.800
You need people who are
feeding them information.

00:38:33.800 --> 00:38:35.690
You need people to
do communications

00:38:35.690 --> 00:38:36.440
and troubleshoot.

00:38:36.440 --> 00:38:39.650
And that needs to be able to
expand or shrink depending

00:38:39.650 --> 00:38:41.490
on the scale of the disaster.

00:38:41.490 --> 00:38:43.160
And that's a little
bit unusual for how

00:38:43.160 --> 00:38:45.070
we would run the day to day.

00:38:45.070 --> 00:38:47.660
And so practicing that
over and over again

00:38:47.660 --> 00:38:51.920
and being prepared on the human
side makes a huge difference.

00:38:51.920 --> 00:38:55.190
Ideally, your systems can
self-heal and defend and things

00:38:55.190 --> 00:38:56.177
like that but--

00:38:56.177 --> 00:38:58.010
GEORGE SADLIER: I mean,
Steve and I both sit

00:38:58.010 --> 00:39:02.480
on our global technical
incident response team dealing

00:39:02.480 --> 00:39:04.250
with the largest events.

00:39:04.250 --> 00:39:07.984
But I remember a good couple
of years ago now, during DRT,

00:39:07.984 --> 00:39:10.400
because DRT, of course, is one
of the most stressful times

00:39:10.400 --> 00:39:11.900
of the incident response team.

00:39:11.900 --> 00:39:15.440
Because we want to
make like this is real.

00:39:15.440 --> 00:39:18.530
But we're taking events
that, in the real world,

00:39:18.530 --> 00:39:21.175
the narrative would
span over a month maybe,

00:39:21.175 --> 00:39:24.740
and we can press it down
to like three or four days.

00:39:24.740 --> 00:39:27.350
And I remember a couple
of days that week where

00:39:27.350 --> 00:39:31.400
I was the incident commander
for the global environment,

00:39:31.400 --> 00:39:37.940
and I think simultaneously I had
three data centers on emergency

00:39:37.940 --> 00:39:43.070
power, one under simulated
terrorist attack,

00:39:43.070 --> 00:39:46.760
the Bay Area was under
water, and something

00:39:46.760 --> 00:39:48.610
was going on in Europe.

00:39:48.610 --> 00:39:50.990
And so I just had
my white board just

00:39:50.990 --> 00:39:54.350
covered in, like, current status
and runtimes of facilities.

00:39:54.350 --> 00:39:58.580
And I've got various
operations leads

00:39:58.580 --> 00:40:00.170
running these
individual incidents

00:40:00.170 --> 00:40:01.910
as I try and keep track of it.

00:40:01.910 --> 00:40:04.340
And because of the simulated
outage at headquarters,

00:40:04.340 --> 00:40:07.055
I supposedly have no
fallback to executives.

00:40:07.055 --> 00:40:10.400
And so it's just like,
we'll keep production up

00:40:10.400 --> 00:40:12.100
as long we can.

00:40:12.100 --> 00:40:15.075
But it was quite the test of our
incident management capability.

00:40:15.075 --> 00:40:17.450
STEVE CARSTENSEN: I mean, the
great thing about that is I

00:40:17.450 --> 00:40:19.908
never have that experience,
because I work in the Bay Area,

00:40:19.908 --> 00:40:22.490
and inevitably, in DRT
week, they tell us,

00:40:22.490 --> 00:40:23.450
oh, you're under water.

00:40:23.450 --> 00:40:24.800
You're part of the disaster.

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:26.600
So the rest of the
world has to take over.

00:40:26.600 --> 00:40:28.391
GEORGE SADLIER: Yeah,
I'm based in Seattle,

00:40:28.391 --> 00:40:31.957
so whenever that happens,
everyone looks at me.

00:40:31.957 --> 00:40:34.040
And one or two of my
colleagues on the East Coast.

00:40:36.482 --> 00:40:38.690
DAVE RENSIN: Okay, I want
to be mindful of time here.

00:40:38.690 --> 00:40:40.367
I want to wrap up with this.

00:40:40.367 --> 00:40:41.950
All right, so John,
we heard from you.

00:40:41.950 --> 00:40:47.180
Steve, I want to know, what is
either the strangest incident

00:40:47.180 --> 00:40:49.400
you have caused, Steve
Carstensen has caused,

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:53.895
or that you've been a part
of, that you can talk about?

00:40:53.895 --> 00:40:54.770
STEVE CARSTENSEN: Oh.

00:40:54.770 --> 00:40:58.440
DAVE RENSIN: I know there are
so many from which to choose.

00:40:58.440 --> 00:41:00.740
STEVE CARSTENSEN: I'm having
a hard time right now.

00:41:00.740 --> 00:41:09.420
Uh-- I mean, the
edge note outage.

00:41:12.330 --> 00:41:15.450
So our edge nodes are
internally culled satellites,

00:41:15.450 --> 00:41:18.030
so the edge node outage is
known internally as Satpocalypse

00:41:18.030 --> 00:41:20.490
because it's when we
destroyed all the satellites.

00:41:20.490 --> 00:41:22.950
That was one of my
favorite outages.

00:41:22.950 --> 00:41:24.810
I'm trying to think
of another one

00:41:24.810 --> 00:41:26.602
that I can actually talk about.

00:41:26.602 --> 00:41:28.020
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:28.020 --> 00:41:29.020
STEVE CARSTENSEN: Sorry?

00:41:29.020 --> 00:41:31.087
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:31.087 --> 00:41:33.420
STEVE CARSTENSEN: That was
actually before my time, sir.

00:41:33.420 --> 00:41:34.692
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:34.692 --> 00:41:35.540
GEORGE SADLIER: I can do mine.

00:41:35.540 --> 00:41:35.970
STEVE CARSTENSEN: All right.

00:41:35.970 --> 00:41:36.660
DAVE RENSIN: Let George go.

00:41:36.660 --> 00:41:37.620
STEVE CARSTENSEN: I don't
know anything about it all.

00:41:37.620 --> 00:41:38.580
George, it's his.

00:41:38.580 --> 00:41:40.038
GEORGE SADLIER: I
was going to say,

00:41:40.038 --> 00:41:43.230
it wasn't an outage per se, but
we had a particularly hilarious

00:41:43.230 --> 00:41:44.950
internal outage.

00:41:44.950 --> 00:41:48.915
So much as you know, we
have a pretty impressive

00:41:48.915 --> 00:41:49.540
infrastructure.

00:41:49.540 --> 00:41:51.956
Got a lot of software engineers
doing a lot of cool stuff,

00:41:51.956 --> 00:41:54.240
but we've got
legacy systems, too.

00:41:54.240 --> 00:41:56.750
To be fair, the company's
only about 15 years old, so--

00:41:56.750 --> 00:41:58.920
well, 20 years now, but--

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:02.460
not that legacy, but we had
one particular internal system

00:42:02.460 --> 00:42:05.490
within the ads environment
where we saw stored session

00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:09.260
state for some front ends.

00:42:09.260 --> 00:42:11.010
Back when we thought
that was a good idea.

00:42:11.010 --> 00:42:13.230
We don't-- general
rule of thumb,

00:42:13.230 --> 00:42:15.565
try to avoid session
state if you ever can.

00:42:15.565 --> 00:42:17.190
But we had session
state, and we needed

00:42:17.190 --> 00:42:19.290
to flush it into the
storage back ends in order

00:42:19.290 --> 00:42:22.759
to ensure that session state
would be retained if we had

00:42:22.759 --> 00:42:24.800
a fault. If there was a
failure of our front end,

00:42:24.800 --> 00:42:27.420
we could keep session
state on a different node.

00:42:27.420 --> 00:42:29.580
And this was ancient,
ancient code.

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:32.340
We have whole RPC
environments now

00:42:32.340 --> 00:42:33.840
which handle internal
communication,

00:42:33.840 --> 00:42:36.700
but this predates all of them.

00:42:36.700 --> 00:42:38.340
So it was a very
primitive system,

00:42:38.340 --> 00:42:39.798
and we'd never
really felt the need

00:42:39.798 --> 00:42:42.456
to upgrade it because it worked.

00:42:42.456 --> 00:42:43.830
And then one day,
the nodes start

00:42:43.830 --> 00:42:46.760
failing on us, the
session state service.

00:42:46.760 --> 00:42:48.500
And we can't figure out why.

00:42:48.500 --> 00:42:50.920
They are just falling
over, globally.

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:53.190
Not all at once,
but sequentially,

00:42:53.190 --> 00:42:55.070
and just patterned
down, and just failing.

00:42:55.070 --> 00:42:57.570
And so we're all frantically
looking, because we're worried.

00:42:57.570 --> 00:43:02.310
It's working, because these
things are multiply redundant,

00:43:02.310 --> 00:43:04.620
objects are getting passed
between nodes, and so

00:43:04.620 --> 00:43:06.810
no user-visible problems.

00:43:06.810 --> 00:43:08.730
But if we lose
many more at once--

00:43:08.730 --> 00:43:10.280
because they restart quickly--

00:43:10.280 --> 00:43:11.530
we're going to have a problem.

00:43:11.530 --> 00:43:12.540
So we're all freaking
out, because we're

00:43:12.540 --> 00:43:14.331
trying to figure out
what on earth is going

00:43:14.331 --> 00:43:17.130
wrong with these things before
it worsens and we actually

00:43:17.130 --> 00:43:17.970
have an outage.

00:43:17.970 --> 00:43:21.540
And we discover
they're all going out

00:43:21.540 --> 00:43:24.270
of memory, which is
weird because they really

00:43:24.270 --> 00:43:26.527
don't do very much.

00:43:26.527 --> 00:43:28.110
And we dig through
the logs, and we're

00:43:28.110 --> 00:43:32.640
having a heck of a time finding
logs because guess what?

00:43:32.640 --> 00:43:34.110
You usually write
logs when you're

00:43:34.110 --> 00:43:37.544
done writing a request,
done processing a request.

00:43:37.544 --> 00:43:39.210
If you crash while
you're processing it,

00:43:39.210 --> 00:43:41.549
you don't always
get a log entry.

00:43:41.549 --> 00:43:42.840
So we're frantically searching.

00:43:42.840 --> 00:43:45.630
We think we've got a query
of death, which is basically

00:43:45.630 --> 00:43:49.560
some combination of
traffic at the front end

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:51.420
is making its way
through and knocking over

00:43:51.420 --> 00:43:53.070
any back end it hits.

00:43:53.070 --> 00:43:56.664
So we're trying dump out traffic
and figure out what's going on.

00:43:56.664 --> 00:43:58.830
And then we notice that,
when they go out of memory,

00:43:58.830 --> 00:44:02.500
they always request precisely
the same amount of memory.

00:44:02.500 --> 00:44:05.519
So it's 4 gig or so of RAM or
whatever they were requesting,

00:44:05.519 --> 00:44:07.560
and it was like it was
taking them out of memory.

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:10.260
It was always exactly
the same number.

00:44:10.260 --> 00:44:11.650
And it was suspicious.

00:44:11.650 --> 00:44:13.770
The number was not
special in any way

00:44:13.770 --> 00:44:16.590
except it was always the same.

00:44:16.590 --> 00:44:18.840
And so we're like, OK, clearly
query of death, clearly

00:44:18.840 --> 00:44:19.500
query of death.

00:44:19.500 --> 00:44:22.230
We're digging around, we can't
figure out what's going on.

00:44:22.230 --> 00:44:25.380
And the failure rate stabilized,
and we can't figure out

00:44:25.380 --> 00:44:26.550
what's going on.

00:44:26.550 --> 00:44:29.040
And then an amazing
intuitive leap.

00:44:29.040 --> 00:44:33.420
One of the engineers on the
team just sits back in his chair

00:44:33.420 --> 00:44:38.160
and says something I won't
repeat on live camera

00:44:38.160 --> 00:44:40.710
and then just writes up
on the board four dashes,

00:44:40.710 --> 00:44:46.330
and on top of them
writes G-E-T space.

00:44:46.330 --> 00:44:50.260
That was the ASCII
coding of the DSML number

00:44:50.260 --> 00:44:52.570
that was being allocated.

00:44:52.570 --> 00:44:55.420
And so what was happening is one
of the early spiders in Google

00:44:55.420 --> 00:45:00.610
production was sweeping through
production, connecting to ports

00:45:00.610 --> 00:45:04.550
and sending a get, as you
do, because most things

00:45:04.550 --> 00:45:07.170
at Google speak
HTTP, unsurprisingly.

00:45:07.170 --> 00:45:08.800
Except these machines didn't.

00:45:08.800 --> 00:45:11.790
They ran a very
primitive protocol,

00:45:11.790 --> 00:45:14.980
and the first 32
bits of the packet

00:45:14.980 --> 00:45:19.450
were the size of the
object being allocated.

00:45:19.450 --> 00:45:22.530
So when you connected to that
port and you said get slash,

00:45:22.530 --> 00:45:26.590
it interpreted the
get space in DSML

00:45:26.590 --> 00:45:28.510
as the size of the
object that followed.

00:45:28.510 --> 00:45:29.410
So of course, the
first thing it did

00:45:29.410 --> 00:45:32.245
was it tried to allocate that
much memory, couldn't, failed.

00:45:35.290 --> 00:45:37.690
So we just sat around the
room laughing our asses off

00:45:37.690 --> 00:45:42.310
for about half an hour before
we pushed an emergency fix

00:45:42.310 --> 00:45:44.272
to input check.

00:45:44.272 --> 00:45:46.480
DAVE RENSIN: All right,
Steve, you've had a reprieve.

00:45:46.480 --> 00:45:47.730
We've got about a minute left.

00:45:47.730 --> 00:45:49.450
STEVE CARSTENSEN: All right.

00:45:49.450 --> 00:45:51.110
So there's too
many to pick from,

00:45:51.110 --> 00:45:55.819
but this is a more recent
event, a year or two ago.

00:45:55.819 --> 00:45:57.610
And I pick it mostly
because I was actually

00:45:57.610 --> 00:45:59.500
on call for this event.

00:45:59.500 --> 00:46:02.770
I'm on call, I got a
huge passel of pages,

00:46:02.770 --> 00:46:07.210
because an IP
address that is used

00:46:07.210 --> 00:46:12.460
for domain resolution external
to Google, one of our DNS-VIPS,

00:46:12.460 --> 00:46:13.930
was being block hold.

00:46:13.930 --> 00:46:16.720
And all of our monitoring
was alerting and saying

00:46:16.720 --> 00:46:18.270
horrible, horrible things.

00:46:18.270 --> 00:46:19.780
And so I get paged.

00:46:19.780 --> 00:46:21.280
I start frantically
trying to debug,

00:46:21.280 --> 00:46:23.810
trying to figure
out what's going on.

00:46:23.810 --> 00:46:26.512
I eventually figure out that
the traffic is successfully

00:46:26.512 --> 00:46:28.720
coming into our backbone,
it's crossing our backbone,

00:46:28.720 --> 00:46:31.480
it's getting to some
of the locations

00:46:31.480 --> 00:46:32.957
where we host this IP address.

00:46:32.957 --> 00:46:34.790
We're looking, we're
looking, we're looking.

00:46:34.790 --> 00:46:39.130
And then eventually, through
a chain that I can't really

00:46:39.130 --> 00:46:41.770
summarize, chain of events,
one of my teammates and I

00:46:41.770 --> 00:46:44.170
figure out that what
is actually happening

00:46:44.170 --> 00:46:48.640
is one of our internal systems
that is a defense system

00:46:48.640 --> 00:46:50.650
to deal with DDoS
attacks has been

00:46:50.650 --> 00:46:53.170
enabled on this particular IP.

00:46:53.170 --> 00:46:55.210
And it's at that point
that we discovered

00:46:55.210 --> 00:46:57.040
that this was
actually a DRT test

00:46:57.040 --> 00:46:59.980
to see whether we would notice
and how fast we would figure it

00:46:59.980 --> 00:47:00.520
out.

00:47:00.520 --> 00:47:02.770
Because it turns out that
the way that this all works,

00:47:02.770 --> 00:47:05.530
this particular IP address
was not actually important.

00:47:05.530 --> 00:47:10.060
Clients just seamlessly failover
to one of the other NS-VIPS,

00:47:10.060 --> 00:47:14.790
and so the impact was zero,
except to my heartbeat.

00:47:14.790 --> 00:47:16.240
DAVE RENSIN: Your sanity, yeah.

00:47:16.240 --> 00:47:17.740
STEVE CARSTENSEN:
It was good times.

00:47:17.740 --> 00:47:19.698
We did figure it out in
what, like, 20 minutes?

00:47:19.698 --> 00:47:20.655
Yeah.

00:47:20.655 --> 00:47:22.030
DAVE RENSIN: OK,
so on that note.

00:47:22.030 --> 00:47:23.650
A quick announcement.

00:47:23.650 --> 00:47:26.290
Remember, everyone, 5
o'clock this evening,

00:47:26.290 --> 00:47:28.750
happy hour here in the 5th Nine
lounge for those of you who

00:47:28.750 --> 00:47:30.650
are in person and at the event.

00:47:30.650 --> 00:47:31.900
We'll be joined by Home Depot.

00:47:31.900 --> 00:47:33.790
They're going to have a bunch
of really interesting things

00:47:33.790 --> 00:47:34.289
to say.

00:47:34.289 --> 00:47:36.190
But just this moment,
please, a round

00:47:36.190 --> 00:47:40.660
of applause for our panelists
John Welsh, Steve Carstensen,

00:47:40.660 --> 00:47:41.700
and George Sadlier.

00:47:41.700 --> 00:47:44.750
[APPLAUSE]

